      "content": "\r\n## What is NYT Strands?\r\n\r\nNYT Strands is a daily word puzzle published by The New York Times. It combines elements of word searches and crossword puzzles into a unique grid-based challenge that has quickly become one of the most popular daily word games.\r\n\r\nEach day, you're presented with a 6×8 grid of letters. Your goal is to find all the theme words hidden in the grid by connecting adjacent letters. Every puzzle has a theme clue, several theme words, and a special Spangram word that spans the entire board.\r\n\r\n## How the Game Works\r\n\r\n### The Grid\r\n\r\nWhen you open the puzzle, you see 48 letters arranged in a 6-column, 8-row grid. Unlike traditional word searches, every single letter belongs to exactly one answer word — there are no leftover or filler letters.\r\n\r\n### The Theme Clue\r\n\r\nAt the top of the puzzle, you'll see a theme clue. This hint tells you what all the theme words have in common. The clue might be straightforward (\"Fruits\") or involve wordplay (\"On a roll\").\r\n\r\n### Finding Words\r\n\r\nConnect adjacent letters (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally) to form words. Words can twist and turn in any direction — they don't have to follow straight lines. Each letter can only be used once per word.\r\n\r\n### Theme Words (Blue)\r\n\r\nWhen you find a word that matches the puzzle's theme, it's highlighted in blue and those letters are marked as \"found.\" There are typically 6-8 theme words per puzzle.\r\n\r\n### The Spangram (Gold)\r\n\r\nThe Spangram is a special word that spans the entire board — it must touch two opposite edges (left-right, top-bottom, or diagonally). It's highlighted in gold and describes the overall theme. Think of it as the category name for all the theme words.\r\n\r\n### Hint System\r\n\r\nFinding valid non-theme words (at least 4 letters long) earns you hint tokens. Every 3 tokens reveals the location of a letter in one of the remaining theme words. This is a built-in help mechanic — use it when you're stuck.\r\n\r\n### Winning\r\n\r\nYou win when you find the Spangram and all theme words. The entire grid will be covered, with every letter belonging to an answer word.\r\n\r\n## Step-by-Step: Your First Puzzle\r\n\r\nHere's how to approach your first Strands puzzle:\r\n\r\n**Step 1:** Read the theme clue. Think about what words might relate to it. Don't rush to the grid yet.\r\n\r\n**Step 2:** Scan the grid for the Spangram. Look for long words that stretch from one edge to the opposite edge. The Spangram describes the theme, so finding it confirms your interpretation.\r\n\r\n**Step 3:** Look for obvious theme words. Start from corners and edges where letter connections are simpler.\r\n\r\n**Step 4:** As you find words, track which letters are \"used.\" The remaining uncovered letters must form the remaining answers.\r\n\r\n**Step 5:** If stuck, find any valid 4+ letter words (even non-theme words) to earn hint tokens. Three tokens reveal a letter.\r\n\r\n**Step 6:** Use elimination. As fewer letters remain, the possible words become easier to identify.\r\n\r\n## Common Beginner Mistakes\r\n\r\n### Mistake 1: Straight-Line Scanning\r\n\r\nThe biggest beginner mistake is looking for words in straight lines only. Strands words snake through the grid, changing direction at every letter. Think of it like tracing a path on a map, not reading text on a page.\r\n\r\n### Mistake 2: Ignoring the Theme Clue\r\n\r\nJumping straight into word-hunting without considering the clue wastes time. The clue tells you what types of words to look for. Always start by thinking about the theme.\r\n\r\n### Mistake 3: Avoiding the Hint System\r\n\r\nMany beginners see hints as \"cheating.\" They're not — they're a designed part of the game. If you've been stuck for 3+ minutes, earn some hint tokens. It's much better than staring at the grid in frustration.\r\n\r\n### Mistake 4: Forgetting the Spangram\r\n\r\nSome beginners focus entirely on finding theme words and forget about the Spangram. The Spangram is often the key to understanding the theme. Finding it early gives you a major advantage.\r\n\r\n### Mistake 5: Not Tracking Used Letters\r\n\r\nAfter finding a word, its letters are \"used up\" — they belong to that word and not to any other. The remaining uncovered letters form the remaining answers. Pay attention to what's left on the board.\r\n\r\n## Where to Play\r\n\r\nThe official NYT Strands puzzle is available at [nytimes.com/games/strands](https://www.nytimes.com/games/strands). A new puzzle is released every day at midnight Eastern Time.\r\n\r\n## Need Help?\r\n\r\nIf you're stuck on today's puzzle, we offer [progressive hints](/strands-hint-today) that let you get just enough help without fully spoiling the answer. You can also learn from past puzzles in our [archive](/strands-hint) or read our [strategy guide](/blog/strands-strategies-guide) for more advanced tips.\r\n\r\nWelcome to the Strands community — and happy puzzling!\r\n",
